{"type": "say", "text": "Maya, I want to add one legal consideration to the renegotiation and restructuring discussion. The commissioning certificate I flagged isn't just a procedural step — it's a condition precedent in the interconnection agreement, which means the system cannot be legally energized until the utility signs off. From a contracting standpoint, if we're renegotiating the purchase price, I'd recommend we also revisit the payment milestones in the purchase agreement so that a meaningful portion of the purchase price is tied to successful commissioning and energization, rather than being due on delivery alone. That way we're not fully out of pocket on a $6 million — or renegotiated — purchase price while waiting for the utility to issue the commissioning certificate. I'd also want to make sure the $400,000 performance bond obligation is clearly allocated in the contract — whether the vendor covers it or we do — since that's a real cash commitment that sits between purchase and energization. I haven't yet added these conditions to the draft approval, but I can do so before the decision is formally recorded so the approval reflects both the bond requirement and the commissioning gate as part of the project's total cost and timeline."}
